 86AS c  Dataset adapted from a compiled dataset from 2015Ma61 by A.A. Sonzogni 
 86AS2c  (NNDC, BNL), Dec 01, 2015, and made available in the XUNDL database.   
 86AS c  2015Ma61: {+86}Ge beam produced in U(p,F),E=50 MeV at the Holifield    
 86AS2c  Radioactive Ion Beam facility (HRIBF) in ORNL, followed by             
 86AS3c  electromagnetic separation of the fission fragments. The radioactive   
 86AS4c  ion beam pf 18% {+86}Ge and 82% {+86}As was implanted on a moving tape 
 86AS5c  collector (MTC). Measured E|g, I|g, |b|g|g-coin, |g|g-coin using four  
 86AS6c  HPGe clover detectors for |g rays and two plastic scintillators for |b 
 86AS7c  radiation. Comparison with shell-model calculations. In an earlier     
 86AS8c  study by 2013Ma22 from the same experimental group at ORNL, T{-1/2} of 
 86AS9c  {+86}Ge decay and energies of two |g rays of 111.7 and 118.9 keV were  
 86ASAc  measured.                                                              
 86AS c  Theory references for T{-1/2} of {+86}Ge decay: 2022Qu03, 2016Sh39,    
 86AS2c  2016So03, 2015Sa14, 2012Ch48, 2005Bo19, 1981Al25.                      
 86AS c  The decay scheme is considered incomplete by the evaluators as         
 86AS2c  at least two strong |g rays remain unassigned and unplaced. In         
 86AS3c  addition, large difference between Q(|b{+-})=9560 {I440} and           
 86AS4c  the highest level at 2917 keV in the present decay scheme allows       
 86AS5c  the possibility of unobserved |g transitions from higher levels        
 86AS cG $From the decay scheme in 2015Ma61, evaluators deduce |g-normalization 
 86AS2cG factor of 0.283 {I51} from I(|g+ce)(97.6|g) + I(|g+ce)(111.8|g) +      
 86AS2cG I(|g+ce)(118.9|g) + I(|g+ce)(178.1|g, for mult=M1,E2,E1) +             
 86AS3cG I(|g)(441.1|g)=64 {I11} from 100-%|b{+-}n (adopted %|b{+-}n=24.5       
 86AS4cG {I12}), and estimated 12% {I11} (2015Ma61) feeding to the three lowest 
 86AS5cG levels: g.s., 7 keV and 21 keV. However, as the decay scheme is        
 86AS6cG incomplete, no meaningful normalization factor can be adopted.         
 86AS cG E(b)$This |g either from |b decay or |bn decay of {+86}As. 2015Ma61    
 86AS2cG state that the 178|g and 441|g most likely originate from the |b decay 
 86AS3cG of {+86}As, but could not be placed in the level scheme.               
 86AS cG E$From 2015Ma61.                                                       
 86AS cG TI$From 2015Ma61, with assumed mult=M1.                                
 86AS cG RI$For low-energy (E|g<200 keV) transitions, 2015Ma61 provided         
 86AS2cG transition intensity. Relative I|g values have been deduced by         
 86AS2cG evaluators using conversion coefficients for assumed mult=M1           
 86AS cG E(d)$|g observed only in coincidence data                              
 86AS cG M(a)$2015Ma61 assumed M1 in contrast to E1 from shell-model            
 86AS2cG considerations, and mult=Q or higher multipolarity for low-energy      
 86AS2cG (E|g|<150 keV) transitions are expected to lead to level half-lives    
 86AS3cG of several hundred ns. In the present level scheme there is no         
 86AS4cG evidence for such long-lived isomeric states. Evaluators note,         
 86AS5cG however, that in Fig. 8 of 2015Ma61, agreement is poor between the     
 86AS6cG experimental levels in the present decay scheme and the theoretical    
 86AS7cG levels from shell-model calculations, and it seems that only the       
 86AS8cG negative-parity levels were calculated from shell model.               
 86AS cL E$From least-squares fit to E|g data.                                  
