 86NB cL E$expected to be a low-spin isomer populated in the                    
 86NB2cL |e decay of {+86}Mo, but no T{-1/2} data are available. Unpublished    
 86NB3cL work on {+86}Nb decay to {+86}Zr (2000DoZV) and systematics of odd-odd 
 86NB4cL nuclides in this mass region suggest existence of a low-spin isomer    
 86NB cL J$(E1) |g from (1+).                                                   
 86NB cL T$not measured. From systematics, 2021Ko07 suggest 20 s                
 86NB  L 0+Y                            56 S     8                           M2?
 86NB cL T$from 1994Sh07 in {+54}Fe({+35}Cl,2pn|g) at E=103 MeV. The half-life  
 86NB2cL is from the decay curve of the Zr K| x ray in coin with                
 86NB3cL 752+915+1003 |g rays in {+86}Zr. From observation of K| x ray in coin  
 86NB4cL with known |g rays of 360, 620, 670, 1039 and 1363 in {+86}Zr, 1994Sh07
 86NB5cL suggest that a high-spin level in {+86}Zr at excitation energy above 3 
 86NB6cL MeV may have been populated by the |e decay of this isomer. However,   
 86NB8cL the existence of this 56-s activity is considered as uncertain by the  
 86NB9cL evaluators since it has not been confirmed in other independent        
 86NBAcL studies. Furthermore, it is possible that the source of {+86}Nb formed 
 86NBBcL by 1994Sh07 is a mixed activity of 88-s, (6+) isomer and a low-spin    
 86NBCcL isomer of unknown half-life populated in {+86}Mo |e decay. No evidence 
 86NBDcL of this isomer was found by 1997Ta10 (2000DoZV) or 2005Ka39.           
